7. Broadly speaking, all the fifteen instances where the corporate veil is lifted disregarding the corporate personality found in the Act itself, mentioned in Chapter 10, are directly or indirectly meant to serve the purpose of conservation and maintenance of the capital of companies. So also the provisions contained in sections 99 and 323 of the Act for creation of reserve liability of members or unlimited liability of all or any of the directors of the company.

The curbs listed above intended to contribute to the maintenance of capital primarily in the interest of creditors, the unsecured creditors in particular, appear to be illusory as those stand more or less nullified by one single counter-measure of empowerment of the shareholders of the company flowing from the very concepts of a company s constitution provided in the Act for the disposition including sale of the whole or substantially the whole of the undertaking(s) owned, merely through an ordinary resolution at a general meeting, behind the creditors, section 293(1)(a). The empowerment in the name of prohibition and restriction regarding political contributions by companies provided under section 293A also belongs to the same genere, may be to a lesser extent.

Reduction of Share Capital [Sections 100, 101 and 105]

13.11-6 Loss or gain inherent in business, which may include capital lost or gained to be in excess, is an incident of corporate business too and this fact is well recognized and accepted in Company Law as capital lost or gained to be in excess of company s needs termed as over-capitalisation , but it does not allow writing down of share capital or a wanton waiver of capital un-paid or withdrawal of paid-up capital or appropriation of company s assets by the corporators, freely, except in the case of unlimited companies. The essential question of reduction of capital is a purely private matter within the discretion of members of the company to be decided by a special resolution at a meeting, if so authorized by its articles, but the method and manner of doing so has to be specific, open and public subject to the confirmation of the Court 1 /Tribunal, section 100. This restriction applies to companies limited by shares or guarantee and shares. The alteration of the memorandum to bring about the reduction of share capital may be in any way chosen by the company, but, three such ways are particularized in the section, namely,

(a) to extinguish or reduce the liability on any of its shares in respect of share capital not paid-up,

(b) cancellation of any paid-up capital which is lost or is unrepresented by available assets, and

(c) return of paid-up capital to the holders of shares which is in excess of the company s wants, either with or without extinguishing or reducing liability on any of the company s shares mentioned in (b) or (c), subject to the interests of the creditors being not prejudiced thereby irrespective of the way in which the capital reduction is sought to be brought about by the members.

Reduction of capital is not a corporate affair left to the discretion of the board of directors behind the members, in the Act, though the shares may be under their control by the company s articles. In the words of Romer, LJ, In re Haematite Steel Co. (1901) 2 Ch. 749, capital is not lost unless it is permanently lost. So, the Court 1/Tribunal may insist in cases where capital loss is taken as the cause of reduction of capital on the company undertaking to create a capital reserve out of all or any realization on account of the lost assets, so that the proceeds are not available for distribution as dividends, and make it a condition of its confirmatory order.

